index 0000000000..9d7efd3ca9
--- /dev/null
+++ b/test/draft/ldp/ldp-setup-failure-publishes-no-session-down.ci
@@ -0,0 +1,64 @@
+# Test: an LDP setup attempt the neighbor rejects is recorded as a failed setup,
+# delayed 15 seconds, and publishes no session event.
+#
+# VALIDATES: spec-single-owner-lifecycle-5-session-end-locals AC-3 and AC-4 --
+#            startSessionForAdj's session-end callback records a setup failure
+#            for a session that never reached OPERATIONAL, and publishes no
+#            SessionDown for it, through the daemon on a real link.
+# PREVENTS:  a setup attempt that never published a session-up publishing a
+#            session-down, which costs an OSPF LDP-sync interface its link and
+#            re-arms its stuck alert (plan/journal/state-read-after-teardown-reset-it.md,
+#            2026-10-02); and a NAK'd Initialization redialled before the RFC 5036
+#            Section 2.5.3 floor.
+#
+# WHY A .ci. internal/plugins/ldp/session_end_test.go drives the same callback
+# over net.Pipe. This test answers whether the daemon does it end to end, with a
+# neighbor that NAKs Ze's Initialization on the wire and an observer plugin that
+# hears the `ldp` namespace through the engine's event delivery.
+#
+# HOW. `ldp/neighbor-link` runs the daemon in its own network namespace, joined
+# by a veth pair to a second one that holds the neighbor's link. The observer
+# plugin `ldp/setup-failure-publishes-no-session-down` is the neighbor and the
+# observer: subscribed to ldp session-up and session-down, it rejects Ze's
+# first Initialization with a fatal Session Rejected/Parameters Max PDU Length
+# Notification and closes the connection. It keeps sending Hellos, requires
+# Ze's next session to come no sooner than 15 seconds after the rejection,
+# brings that one to operational, and ends it.
+#
+# The second session is the positive control: its session-up and session-down
+# reach the observer, so silence for the rejected attempt is Ze's and not a
+# deaf observer. Delivery to one plugin keeps Ze's emit order, so the history
+# MUST be exactly session-up, session-down. The decision log names the delay.
+#
+# STATUS: a Linux/QEMU test. The fixture creates network namespaces and a veth
+# pair, which needs root (`./le test qemu ci test test/ldp/ldp-setup-failure-publishes-no-session-down.ci`).
+option=needs-linux:caps=net-admin
+option=env:var=ze.log.ldp:value=info
+option=timeout:value=120s
+
+tmpfs=ldp.conf:terminator=EOF_CONF
+plugin {
+	external ldp-neighbor {
+		run "le test fixture ldp/setup-failure-publishes-no-session-down netns zlf$PORT-n"
+		encoder json
+	}
+}
+
+ldp {
+	lsr-id 10.99.0.2
+	transport-address 10.99.0.2
+	hello-interval 5
+	hello-hold-time 15
+	keepalive-time 60
+	interfaces ldpz0
+}
+EOF_CONF
+
+cmd=foreground:seq=1:exec=le test fixture ldp/neighbor-link netns zlf$PORT run ze start ldp.conf:timeout=115s
+expect=exit:code=0
+expect=stderr:contains=ldp: discovery started on interface
+expect=stderr:pattern=session setup failed, next attempt delayed.*peer=10\.99\.0\.1:0.*delay=15s
+expect=stderr:pattern=OK: the rejected attempt published no session event.*events \[session-up session-down\]
+reject=stderr:pattern=ZE-OBSERVER-FAIL
+reject=stderr:pattern=panic
+reject=stderr:pattern=fatal error
diff --git a/test/draft/l2tp/session-clear-leaves-no-subscriber.ci b/test/draft/l2tp/session-clear-leaves-no-subscriber.ci
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000..e7a8177238
--- /dev/null
+++ b/test/draft/l2tp/session-clear-leaves-no-subscriber.ci
@@ -0,0 +1,133 @@
+# Test: an operator clear of a live PPP session leaves nothing behind.
+# QUESTION:  when the operator clears one L2TP session that has its address and
+#            is up, does the daemon end it in every place that held it: the
+#            session list, the subscriber registry, and the RADIUS accounting
+#            record, closed once?
+# VALIDATES: spec-single-owner-lifecycle-5-session-end-locals AC-8 (end state
+#            after an operator teardown: no subscriber entry, no accounting
+#            session left open, a Stop for every Start) and AC-9 for the two
+#            readers this test can observe (RADIUS accounting sends exactly one
+#            Stop, the subscriber bridge removes its entry), through the real
+#            entry points: a LAC over UDP, kernel PPP, `clear l2tp session id`
+#            over SSH, a RADIUS server on the wire. User story 3.
+# PREVENTS:  a clear that leaves the subscriber listed, an accounting session a
+#            late event started after the teardown (a second Acct-Session-Id),
+#            a Stop that never reaches the server, or a Stop sent twice.
+#
+# Why not a unit test: the unit tests in internal/component/l2tp
+# (session_event_order_test.go) and authradius (acct_order_test.go) prove each
+# half in isolation over a test bus. Only the daemon joins the reactor, the
+# bridge, the accounting plugin and the CLI, so only a .ci proves an operator
+# reaches the end state.
+#
+# What this test does NOT prove: the race the order ticket fixes, a teardown
+# overtaking the SessionIPAssigned or SessionUp of the same session. No daemon
+# run can force that interleaving (the spec says so of its unit tests' seam).
+# Here the session is up, and seen up, before the clear: this is the ordinary
+# clear's end state, not the ordering. The ordering is the unit tests' job.
+#
+# Every assertion is on state, never on time. The peer waits, before the
+# clear, until the Accounting-Start is at the RADIUS server, `show l2tp
+# sessions` lists the session with its address, and `show subscriber` lists it
+# active: those are the positive controls, so an empty list after the clear is
+# not a broken command. After the clear it reads each view ONCE, with no wait:
+# teardownSessionByID delivers the session-down to every reader before it
+# sends the CDN and before the command answers, so the state the answer leaves
+# is the end state.
+#
+# The accounting assertion is the COUNT of records and their shared
+# Acct-Session-Id, never their order on the wire. The Start is sent from the
+# accounting task's goroutine and the Stop at once on teardown, so a teardown
+# right after the address can put the Stop on the wire first
+# (plan/journal/late-write-lands-on-the-successor.md, 2026-11-12, owned by
+# plan/release/fixes/spec-radius-acct-start-after-stop.md). This test takes
+# that race out of what it asserts by seeing the Start at the server before it
+# issues the clear.
+#
+# A session that reaches an assigned IPv4 needs kernel PPPoL2TP, which needs
+# CAP_NET_ADMIN, so this test is needs-linux and runs in QEMU as root. It SKIPS
+# on an unprivileged dev host. ze.l2tp.skip-kernel-probe is deliberately NOT
+# set, as in test/l2tp/radius-acct-wire.ci, whose LAC half and RADIUS mock this
+# test reuses (internal/test/fixture/tunnel_fixture_l2tp_ppp.go,
+# tunnel_fixture_l2tp_radius.go).
+#
+# Ports: $PORT is the L2TP listener, $PORT2 the RADIUS mock (auth and
+# accounting), $PORT3 the daemon's SSH server, which the peer's `ze cli`
+# reaches through the three ze.ssh.* variables below.
+
+stdin=config:terminator=EOF_CONF
+system {
+    authentication {
+        # bcrypt cost-4 hash of "testpass".
+        user admin {
+            password "$2a$04$UlwuiuH82Unfsq.XEMPGJeDkXwbm3KW.nvVaVXOd/JeFK8VjMjrQO"
+        }
+    }
+}
+l2tp {
+    enabled true
+    shared-secret s3cr3t
+    auth-method chap-md5
+    auth {
+        radius {
+            nas-identifier lns1
+            timeout 5
+            retries 2
+            server mock {
+                address 127.0.0.1
+                port $PORT2
+                accounting-port $PORT2
+                shared-key testing123
+            }
+        }
+    }
+    pool {
+        ipv4 {
+            gateway 10.99.8.1
+            start 10.99.8.10
+            end 10.99.8.10
+        }
+    }
+}
+environment {
+    l2tp {
+        server main {
+            ip 127.0.0.1
+            port $PORT
+        }
+    }
+    ssh {
+        enabled true
+        server main {
+            ip 127.0.0.1
+            port $PORT3
+        }
+    }
+}
+EOF_CONF
+
+option=needs-linux:caps=net-admin
+option=env:var=ze.log.l2tp:value=debug
+option=env:var=ze.log.l2tp.auth.radius:value=debug
+option=env:var=TEST_SECRET:value=s3cr3t
+option=env:var=ze.ssh.username:value=admin
+option=env:var=ze.ssh.password:value=testpass
+option=env:var=ze.ssh.port:value=$PORT3
+
+cmd=background:seq=1:exec=le test fixture l2tp/session-clear-leaves-no-subscriber/radius $PORT2 testing123
+cmd=background:seq=2:exec=ze -:stdin=config
+# 120s bounds the peer's own five waits, each a share of this budget, so a
+# stall ends with the peer naming the state that never came. The happy path
+# takes seconds.
+cmd=foreground:seq=3:exec=le test fixture l2tp/session-clear-leaves-no-subscriber $PORT:timeout=120s:exit=0
+expect=exit:code=0
+expect=stdout:contains=OK: CHAP Success received
+expect=stdout:contains=OK: IPCP opened, ze assigned 10.99.8.10
+expect=stdout:pattern=OK: Accounting-Start with Acct-Session-Id \S+ reached the RADIUS server before the clear
+expect=stdout:pattern=OK: show l2tp sessions lists session [0-9]+ with address 10\.99\.8\.10
+expect=stdout:contains=OK: show subscriber lists an active l2tp subscriber with address 10.99.8.10
+expect=stdout:pattern=OK: clear l2tp session id [0-9]+ answered status sent
+expect=stdout:pattern=OK: the RADIUS server holds one Accounting-Start and one Accounting-Stop for Acct-Session-Id \S+, the Stop with Acct-Terminate-Cause 6
+expect=stdout:contains=OK: show l2tp sessions lists no session after the clear
+expect=stdout:contains=OK: show subscriber lists no subscriber after the clear, total 0
+expect=stdout:pattern=OK: ze sent a CDN for session [0-9]+
diff --git a/test/draft/reload/ddos-flowspec-parent-removed-withdraws.ci b/test/draft/reload/ddos-flowspec-parent-removed-withdraws.ci
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000..298419ce9d
--- /dev/null
+++ b/test/draft/reload/ddos-flowspec-parent-removed-withdraws.ci
@@ -0,0 +1,169 @@
+# Test: deleting the PARENT `ddos` config block withdraws the live FlowSpec
+# rule from the BGP peer during the reload, and the withdrawal is the one the
+# config apply sends.
+#
+# QUESTION: does a reload that deletes the whole `ddos` block, with a FlowSpec
+#           rule announced, put the withdrawal of exactly that rule on the
+#           peer's wire before the plugin is stopped? A unit test cannot decide
+#           it: the empty-body apply comes from the reload's ancestor match
+#           (plugin/server/reload.go rootHasChanges), the plugin then sends its
+#           update-route over the transport the daemon gives an internal plugin,
+#           and the bytes are the BGP engine's. The unit pair test
+#           (flowspec/exit_test.go) runs none of the three.
+#
+# VALIDATES: spec-single-owner-lifecycle-5-session-end-locals AC-12 and A-7 --
+#            the reload delivers ddos-flowspec an apply with its section absent
+#            (replaceResponder, configured false -> withdrawForConfig), the log
+#            carries "the ddos flowspec section was removed, withdrawing the
+#            announcement" (responder.go withdrawSectionRemoved), and the peer
+#            receives the MP_UNREACH_NLRI for the NLRI it was announced.
+# PREVENTS:  the parent-block removal leaving the rule at the peer with no plugin
+#            left to withdraw it, and a regression where only the exit path
+#            (the `bye` handler, register.go stopResponder) withdraws: that path
+#            would not log the section-removed line, which is expected below.
+#
+# WHICH PATH WITHDRAWS. The apply runs before the stop (reload.go reloadConfig:
+# verify and apply, then the deferred stopCollectedProcesses), so the apply's
+# withdrawForConfig sends the withdrawal and the `bye` handler then finds
+# nothing live (stopResponder returns on !active, AC-11's survivor). The two
+# rejects below refuse the exit path's failure lines. That the `bye` handler
+# sends NOTHING when the apply withdrew is not judged here: the peer accepts a
+# second identical withdrawal in seq 2 as well as one, so that survivor is
+# TestByeWithNothingAnnouncedSendsNothing's to prove.
+#
+# THE WIRE. The blackhole fallback announces the detector's host prefix with no
+# other component (responder.go renderFlowspecCommand over buildMatch of the
+# AttackDetected target, characterize.go characterizeTarget): FlowSpec NLRI
+# 06 01 20 CB007109, length 6, type 1 destination prefix, /32, 203.0.113.9. The
+# announcement carries the traffic-rate extended community at rate 0, which is
+# discard (RFC 8955 Section 7.1, type 0x80 subtype 0x06, 2-octet AS 0, IEEE
+# float 0). The withdrawal carries the same NLRI in MP_UNREACH_NLRI.
+#
+# WHY THE VICTIM IS REMOTE, WHY blackhole-fallback: test/plugin/ddos-announce-rate-limit.ci.
+# hold-down and max-mitigation-duration are past the run, so neither the leak
+# probe nor the cap can account for the withdrawal: the flood is stopped only
+# after the reload, and the reload is the only remover left.
+#
+# STATUS: a Linux/QEMU integration test; NOT executable on the darwin dev host.
+#   net-admin builds the transit veth pair; bpf is what lets traffic-usage name
+#   the victim (internal/plugins/trafficusage/attach_linux.go).
+option=needs-linux:caps=net-admin,bpf
+# Serialize against every other ddos test: they all flood the same interface
+# set (rationale: test/plugin/ddos-direction.ci).
+option=exclusive:group=ddos-flood
+option=env:var=ze.log.ddos.detect:value=info
+option=env:var=ze.log.ddos.flowspec:value=info
+option=timeout:value=150s
+
+stdin=peer:terminator=EOF_PEER
+option=tcp_connections:value=1
+# The peer never closes: a closed session would drop the rule by itself and
+# make the withdrawal unobservable.
+option=linger:value=true
+# seq 1: the announcement of 203.0.113.9/32 with traffic-rate 0 (discard).
+expect=bgp:conn=1:seq=1:announced=060120CB007109:attribute=16,8006000000000000
+# seq 2: the withdrawal of the same NLRI, in MP_UNREACH_NLRI.
+expect=bgp:conn=1:seq=2:withdrawn=060120CB007109
+EOF_PEER
+
+stdin=ze-bgp:terminator=EOF_CONF
+ddos {
+	detect {
+		enabled true
+		absolute-floor 1000
+		confirm-duration 1
+		clear-consecutive-checks 100
+		startup-grace 0
+		baseline-window 10
+		check-interval 1
+		characterize-enable false
+	}
+	observe {
+		incident-ring-size 100
+	}
+	flowspec {
+		response-level enforce
+		action discard
+		blackhole-fallback true
+		max-mitigation-duration 3600
+		hold-down 3600
+	}
+}
+
+traffic {
+	usage {
+		enabled true
+		track-ip true
+		interfaces {
+			interface zdd0p {
+				enabled true
+			}
+			interface zdd0 {
+				enabled true
+			}
+		}
+	}
+}
+
+bgp {
+	filter {
+		import {
+			final accept
+		}
+		export {
+			final accept
+		}
+	}
+	peer peer1 {
+		connection {
+			remote {
+				ip 127.0.0.1
+			}
+			local {
+				ip 127.0.0.2
+				accept false
+			}
+		}
+		session {
+			asn {
+				local 65000
+				remote 65001
+			}
+			router-id 1.2.3.4
+			family {
+				ipv4/unicast { prefix { maximum 10000; } }
+				ipv4/flow { prefix { maximum 10000; } }
+			}
+			capability {
+				graceful-restart disable
+			}
+		}
+
+		# The responder's update-route is refused for a process no peer
+		# attaches with send [ update ] (reactor/send_permission.go). The
+		# reload keeps this block byte-identical, so the peer is not re-added.
+		attach process ddos-flowspec {
+			send [ update ]
+		}
+	}
+}
+
+plugin {
+	external ddos-flowspec-parent-removed-probe {
+		run "le test fixture plugin/ddos-flowspec-parent-removed-driver"
+		encoder json
+	}
+}
+EOF_CONF
+
+cmd=foreground:seq=1:exec=le test fixture plugin/ddos-transit-forward-drop-setup
+cmd=background:seq=2:exec=le test peer --port $PORT:stdin=peer
+cmd=foreground:seq=3:exec=ze --plugin ze.bgp-nlri-flowspec -:stdin=ze-bgp:timeout=145s
+expect=exit:code=0
+expect=stderr:contains=FLOWSPEC-ANNOUNCED 203.0.113.9
+expect=stderr:contains=FLOWSPEC-WITHDRAWAL-SENT 203.0.113.9
+expect=stderr:contains=ddos-flowspec: announced
+expect=stderr:contains=ddos-flowspec: the ddos flowspec section was removed, withdrawing the announcement
+expect=stderr:contains=ddos-flowspec: withdrawn
+reject=stderr:contains=ddos-flowspec: withdraw failed
+reject=stderr:contains=ddos-flowspec: the FlowSpec rule could not be withdrawn on the way out
diff --git a/test/draft/plugin/ddos-flowspec-orphan-doctor.ci b/test/draft/plugin/ddos-flowspec-orphan-doctor.ci
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000..0f364c2981
--- /dev/null
+++ b/test/draft/plugin/ddos-flowspec-orphan-doctor.ci
@@ -0,0 +1,326 @@
+# Test: a FlowSpec rule recorded by a run that ended without withdrawing it, and
+# not withdrawn by the next start, is reported by `ze doctor` with the victim and
+# the plugin restart as its fix; once the cause of the failed withdrawal is
+# corrected, the fix doctor printed withdraws the rule, removes the record, and
+# clears the report.
+#
+# QUESTION: when the daemon holds a FlowSpec record that its running responder
+#           does not own, does `ze doctor` through the daemon name it under
+#           doctor-ddos-flowspec-orphan-announcement, with the victim prefix and
+#           `ze request plugin restart ddos-flowspec` as the fix, and does
+#           running that fix put the withdrawal on a peer's wire and leave
+#           doctor reporting nothing? A unit test cannot decide it: the record
+#           crosses a daemon death in the disk store, the report travels from
+#           the plugin's runtime check through the daemon's doctor, and the
+#           restart is the daemon's own (handlePluginRestart -> restartPlugin).
+#
+# VALIDATES: spec-single-owner-lifecycle-5-session-end-locals AC-16 (the runtime
+#            half: "a start-time withdrawal that failed", and "the report clears
+#            when the record is removed"), user story 5, and the check's Fix
+#            (ai/rules/user-facing-errors.md, "the exact command that fixes
+#            it"): the command is taken from doctor's own output and run, never
+#            retyped.
+# PREVENTS:  a recorded rule invisible to `ze doctor`, and a fix that leaves the
+#            report standing after the operator ran it.
+#
+# HOW THE DAEMON REACHES THE STATE, with nothing written by hand:
+#   1. The first daemon announces the blackhole-fallback rule for the remote
+#      victim, and the plugin records it (responder.go announce -> Record). The
+#      flood driver waits for the record itself, read back through the daemon
+#      (`show data cat`): the live flag is published before the record is
+#      written, so it is no barrier.
+#   2. The first daemon is KILLED. A killed process withdraws nothing, and the
+#      record stays in the disk store (docs/guide/ddos-mitigation.md, "What
+#      each end of the plugin does"). A SIGTERM would not do: an internal
+#      plugin's stop races the engine's teardown, and a withdrawal that still
+#      reaches the engine removes the record.
+#   3. The second daemon starts on a config that keeps `ddos flowspec` but
+#      whose peer no longer attaches ddos-flowspec. The start-time withdrawal
+#      (withdrawRecordedAnnouncement) is refused by the send permission
+#      (reactor/send_permission.go filterPermittedPeers), so the record stays
+#      and the running responder announces nothing: the runtime check
+#      (doctor.go answerOrphanRuntimeCheck) reports it.
+#   4. The cause is corrected: the operator restores the attach block
+#      (fix-cause.sh copies running.conf over running2.conf and asks for a
+#      reload). A changed attach block travels through RemovePeer and AddPeer
+#      (reactor/delivery_graph.go), so the session to the peer restarts as
+#      connection 3, and the script waits until it is established: a
+#      withdrawal sent before that reaches no peer and fails the same way.
+#   5. The fix doctor printed is run as printed (run-fix.sh). The restarted
+#      plugin's start withdraws the recorded rule through the restored attach
+#      and removes the record (withdrawRecordedAnnouncement), and doctor then
+#      reports nothing (plugin/ddos-flowspec-orphan-cleared).
+#
+# WHAT THE PEER ASSERTS. Connection 1 (first daemon) receives the announcement
+# of 203.0.113.9/32 with traffic-rate 0. Connection 2 (second daemon, no attach)
+# receives no FlowSpec UPDATE: the refused start-time withdrawal sends nothing.
+# Connection 3 (the session the reload restarted) receives the withdrawal the
+# restarted plugin sent. That session never held the rule, so this proves the
+# fix reaches the wire, not what a real peer does with a withdrawal for an NLRI
+# it does not hold (the spec's A-9, which ze-peer does not model).
+#
+# STATUS: a Linux/QEMU integration test; NOT executable on the darwin dev host.
+#   net-admin builds the transit veth pair; bpf lets traffic-usage name the
